===Etymology 2===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|hi|चेला}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ˈt͡ʃeɪ.lə/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-chela2.wav|a=Southern England}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|s|chele}}

# A [[pupil]] or [[disciple]], especially in [[Hinduism]]. {{century|19}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1981|author=w:William Irwin Thompson|title=The Time Falling Bodies Take to Light: Mythology, Sexuality and the Origins of Culture|publisher=Rider/Hutchinson & Co.|location=London|page=184|passage=In fact, Gilgamesh's instructions are still-valid directions from the ''guru'' to the '''''chela'''''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1987|author=Phirozshah Dorabji Mehta|title=The Heart of Religion|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=gor7mhCDzAkC&pg=PA36&dq=%22chela%22&hl=en&ei=PNgNTt_DHq7LmAW91-HKDg&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=9&ved=0CFAQ6AEwCDgK#v=onepage&q=%22chela%22&f=false|page=36
|passage=The guru had insight. He understood his '''chela'''. The relationship between a guru and his '''chela''' was as a father to a son, as a whole person to a whole person, individual and unique.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|title=Warrior of Light: The Life of Nicholas Roerich: Artist, Himalayan Explorer, and Visionary|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=4aB_qCOhJroC&pg=PA52&dq=%22chela%22+guru&hl=en&ei=quANTtn2OKzomAWDqOWrDg&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=5&ved=0CD0Q6AEwBDgU#v=onepage&q=%22chela%22%20guru&f=false|page=52
|passage=The guru-'''chela''' relationship was a theme in many of Nicholas's paintings. In ''Pearl of Searching'', the guru is looking thoughtfully at a pearl necklace with his '''chela'''. This symbolizes the "pearl of great price" that gives purpose to life.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Michael Bertiaux|title=The Voudon Gnostic Workbook: Expanded Edition|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=PHDtPvR3ABgC&pg=PA455&dq=%22chela%22+guru&hl=en&ei=S90NTr3BDKHomAXGq-zJDg&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=9&ved=0CFcQ6AEwCA#v=onepage&q=%22chela%22%20guru&f=false|page=455
|passage=This, of course, is done by the guru as the result of a long process in which his consciousness must merge with that of the '''chela''' and wherein the '''chela''' is absorbed essentially into the guru.}}

